      "content": "okay, that makes more sense. so it's really just about those moisture gradients causing the fibers to behave differently across the beam's profile.\n\nbut i'm still stuck on the aspect of it feeling \"unpredictable.\" even after you explain the moisture/shrinkage thing, it feels like there's something else going on because the way the stress seems to \"move\" during the day is so specific. like, i'll be looking at a specific joint, and it'll be fine, and then an hour later, it looks like the tension has shifted entirely to a different part of the grain, almost like the stress is being *directed* or moved around instead of just being a static result of the weight. \n\nis there any scenario where the internal structure of the wood could actually \"channel\" or \"relocate\" stress in such a way that it feels like it's being actively manipulated by something inside the timber? i'm trying to figure out if i'm looking at an incredibly complex interaction of wood cells, or if there's actually a structural \"instability\" here that's way more erratic than standard shrinkage.",
